<p id="p2">Long-time readers may recall that I used to <a href="https://www.macstories.net/roundups/my-favorite-imessage-apps-and-sticker-packs-of-2016/" rel="noopener noreferrer">regularly cover Messages sticker packs</a>. As sticker popularity “settled,” I retired from that beat, but I thought I should come back for today’s news that <a href="https://apple.co/4yWVaZg" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ariana Grande</a> is the first ever music artist to release an Messages sticker pack.</p>
<p id="p3">The stickers are being released in collaboration with <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/shazam-find-music-concerts/id284993459" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shazam</a>, Apple’s music recognition app, to mark the release of Grande’s new album <a href="https://apple.co/4pN0EkO" rel="noopener noreferrer">petal</a>. The implementation is interesting. According to Apple:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote4"><p>
  Fans can access the sticker pack by using the Shazam app to identify any Ariana Grande song and tapping the special message that appears on the song result or by adding the collection to their emoji keyboard directly in the Messages app. Once downloaded, the stickers can be used in Messages, FaceTime, or any app where emoji keyboard is available.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p5">That in-app link takes you to the Messages corner of the App Store where they’re featured as a Shazam Special Event. You can also access the sticker pack from Shazam’s App Store listing.</p>
<p id="p6">I love seeing Shazam try new things to celebrate artists and their music, and stickers are a fun way to share your excitement about a new release.</p>
<p id="p7">Fun Fact: My Messages sticker situation has never quite recovered from downloading hundreds of sticker packs back in the day. Some features just aren’t meant to be loved so much.</p>
<p id="p8">Also, I am so back.</p>
